  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nA few showers have formed on radar this afternoon, currently across \nfar eastern portions of the Cumberland Plateau. A rumble of thunder \nis possible in this activity as it moves eastwards. Tonight, \nshowers, with maybe a rumble of thunder, will progress from the west \nto the east, and bring light rainfall to mainly a southwestern to \nnortheastern portion of the area. It's conceivable that those \nclosest to, or in, North Carolina will remain dry. \n\nTomorrow, likely towards noon or early afternoon, we will see a \ntransition occur between the morning showers across the north \ndissipating, and scattered showers and storms developing across \nGeorgia and North Carolina. The mountains of far East TN and \nsouthwest NC have the best chances to see rainfall from the \nafternoon and early evening activity. Finally, Sunday is likely to \nsee steadier rainfall for much of the day, especially across far \neastern and southeastern counties bordering NC, as upper jet support \nhelps fuel stratiform rain. Total rain amounts are reasonable at\n1-2 inches, and I think flood risk remains very low overall. \nGuidance has been less enthusiastic about the potential for \nheavier rain rates on Sunday, but if the better forcing arrives, \nthen we would see more moderate rain rates for a sustained period.\nThe best chances of exceeding 2\" through this weekend exist along\nthe northern Cumberland Plateau (for rain now and tomorrow \nmorning primarily), and across our most southern to eastern \ncounties such as Marion, Hamilton to Clay and up to Unicoi (for \nrain tomorrow afternoon and on Sunday primarily). \n\nAs we enter next week we will enter a rather benign weather \npattern, with a strong polar jet north near the Canadian border \nand a weaker subtropical jet across the southern US. End result, \nshy of any cold air dam developments, will be temperatures \nhovering in the upper 70s each day next week for highs, 40s/50s \nfor lows, and dry weather. Also known as fall!",
